## 3. Mapping E-families to your forecasts

**Structure is free; only values need forecasting.** The factors, constants, scale, RPN operators, and *which* E-IDs appear all come straight from AEMO's published pre-dispatch/PASA formulation — you never estimate them. Your only job is each E-term's forward **value**.

**The coherence insight:** every E value is a (near-)deterministic function of a small common state — demand, temperature, wind, solar — that your price simulation already produces. So don't forecast RHS separately from price. Evaluate *both* off the same simulated state; each draw then yields internally consistent (demand, weather, price, RHS, binding-set) tuples.

| E-family | AEMO source | Your forecast analogue | Method | Forecastability |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ratings — static | Workbook season/ToD | Published TER calendar | Deterministic lookup by season/hour | **High** (deterministic) |
| Ratings — dynamic | Real-time weather | Temperature (+wind) model | Rating curve `f(T, wind)`; share latent T with price sim | Medium |
| Ratings — hand-dress/outage | Operational advice | Outage schedule | Scenario input, not a forecast | Low (discrete) |
| Area / zone demand | SCADA → forecast | Your regional demand path, disaggregated | `area = share_a(season, ToD) × region_demand` | **High** (region demand already modelled) |
| Non-sched wind | AWEFS | Your wind model | Expected output given wind state | Medium |
| Non-sched / rooftop solar | ASEFS | Distributed-PV model | `diurnal × seasonal × clear-sky × cloud` | Medium-High |

