## 5. Design decisions, caveats, open questions

- **Use the PASA/pre-dispatch formulation, never dispatch.** ST/MT PASA drop A, S, I types entirely; pre-dispatch recasts flow terms as E-demand-sensitivity terms — so the forward RHS is, by construction, almost all forecastable inputs. Anchor there.
- **Sensitivities are read, not estimated.** Demand-transfer factors are constraint- and orientation-specific and published in the formulation. Re-harvest whenever AEMO reissues the constraint (they move with topology).
- **Outage state dominates.** Getting the constraint-set invocation *distribution* right matters more than precision on any single E-value. Invest in L4.
- **Ratings jump discretely.** EMS-override near-term + outage/hand-dress → model as scenario inputs, don't smooth.
- **Temperature is your one genuinely new module.** Static ratings are a calendar; demand you already have. The new build is a temperature (and, for some elements, wind/irradiance) model for dynamic ratings — and it doubles as a demand and renewable driver, so it earns its keep. (Eydeland–Wolyniec's temperature term-structure models are directly usable.)
- **Semi- vs non-scheduled.** Only non-scheduled wind/solar are E-terms; semi-scheduled units are LHS `T`-terms whose UIGF belongs in dispatch, not the RHS.
- **Open — resolution.** At 5-min, dynamic ratings and solar have strong intraday structure worth carrying; area-demand shares can stay coarser (30-min/hourly) and be interpolated. Decide per family.
- **Open — coupling depth.** Simplest: E-providers are deterministic functions of your existing state. Richer: add rating/wind/solar forecast *error* as extra stochastic factors (matters for tail congestion and cap payoffs). Start deterministic; add error only on terms your sensitivity analysis flags as binding-critical.
